  ],
  "text": "On one planet, the rain is molten glass, and it falls sideways. From far away it looks like a calm blue marble, almost like Earth. But that blue is not ocean. It is a sky full of tiny glass grains. Winds there move faster than a rifle bullet, so nothing ever falls straight down. And it is not even the strangest world we have found. One planet hugs its star so tightly that a year there lasts a few hours. Another may be built around a core of crystallised carbon. One is being eaten alive, stretched into a glowing tail like a comet. We have now confirmed more than five thousand planets around other stars. Almost none of them look anything like our neighbourhood. Out there, glass rain is ordinary. We are the strange ones. But you do not have to leave home for strange. Look at Venus.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro"
}
